  "query_preview": "..\n\nSYNOPTIC SETUP:\n\nThe low pressure system that brought rain to Montana this past\nweekend is exiting eastward ahead of an upper-level ridge building\nover the state. The developing ridge will result in cloud \nceilings scattering out and skies becoming fair by this \nevening.\n\nThe ridge will remain anchored over the northern Great Plains\nregion through tomorrow afternoon, producing southwesterly flow\naloft and subsequent warmer temperatures across NE Montana. A \nshortwave trough will move across northern Wyoming and eastern\nMontana tomorrow night into Wednesday morning. Low-level moisture\nand instability associated with the trough could potentially \ninduce rain showers and thunderstorms (to a much lesser extent) in\nareas SE of the Missouri river between early morning and mid-\nafternoon Wednesday. Isolated thunderstorms will likely be \nlimited to regions SE of the Yellowstone River.\n\nThursday and Friday will have widespread afternoon temperatures \nin the low to mid 80s as ridging resumes following the passage of \nthe shortwave trough. Highest temperatures of the week trend \nwarmer on Thursday as of current model runs, suggesting a quick \nexit of the ridge Friday evening ahead of an approaching upper- \nlevel trough.\n \nAlthough timing discrepancies are currently present between the\nGFS and ECMWF, the trough is expected to begin digging over \neastern Montana between Friday evening and Saturday afternoon.\nVorticity advection is particularly significant ahead of this \ntrough according to current model guidance. However, the vast \nmajority of present low-level moisture is indicated to track \nnorth, potentially resulting in an absence of widespread \nprecipitation. As model guidance develops, forecasted \nprecipitation could change.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nWinds Friday night and Saturday associated with the upper-level \ntrough will need to be monitored, as the NBM/GFS/ECMWF could \npotentially be washing higher speeds out. Strong frontogenesis \nfrom sinking air on the cold side of the low is trending stronger,\nmeaning this event will need to be watched for wind headlines.\n\n\nDON",
